qrt(3))= 4 sqrt(3) ± sqrt(3) sqrt(13)= sqrt(3)(4 ± sqrt(13))\n\nBut R must be positive. Now check which solution is feasible.\n\nFirst solution: R= [4 + sqrt(13)] sqrt(3)\n\nSecond solution: R= [4 - sqrt(13)] sqrt(3)\n\nsqrt(13)≈3.605, so 4 - sqrt(13)≈0.395>0, so [4 - sqrt(13)] sqrt(3)≈0.395*1.732≈0.686, which is positive.\n\nFirst solution: 4 + sqrt(13)=7.605, so R≈7.605*1.732≈13.19, which is larger than 6/sqrt(3)≈3.464. But previously our chord AE=6 gives R=2√3≈3.464. So first solution R≈13.19 would make AE=2R sin(theta/2)=2*13.19*sin(60/2)=≈26.38*sin(30)=26.38*0.5≈13.19, which contradicts AE=6. Thus, first solution invalid.\n\nTherefore, the feasible solution is R= [4 - sqrt(13)] sqrt(3)≈0.686. Then AB=6 -2R≈6 -1.372≈4.628. AD= R≈0.686. DE= R≈0.686, but DE=6 - AD≈6 -0.686≈5.314. But AB + AD=4.628 +0.686≈5.314=DE, which matches.\n\nSo AB≈4.628, AD≈0.686, DE≈5.314, and AE=6 as required.\n\nSo, now we need to find the area of triangle ABC. Since triangle ABC is cyclic, we can use formula Area= (ab sin C)/2. If we can find sides AB, BC, and angle BAC. Alternatively, using formula with two sides and the included angle.\n\nGiven angle BAD=60 degrees, and that the triangle is cyclic.\n\nAlternatively, use coordinate geometry. Let me set point A at (0,0), O at (R,0) where O is center. Point E is at (R sin(theta), theta=120 degrees). Wait, no.\n\nCoordinates approach:\n\nGiven point A at (0,0), point O at (0,0)? No, if triangle ABC is with AB= c=2R. Wait, point A is at (R,0) if AB=2R.\n\nWait, must reset the coordinate system properly.\n\nAssuming point A at (0,0), which is on the circle. Let me set the coordinate system so that point B is at (R,0), radius R, point C somewhere on the circle. Midpoint D of BC is ((x_B +x_C)/2, (y_B + y_C)/2). Then, line AD has slope (y_D)/x_D. The extended line AD meets the circle again at E. AE=6.\n\nGiven that chord AE=6, then as before, R is calculated as 6/(2 sin(theta/2))=6/(2 sin 60)=6/(sqrt(3))=2 sqrt(3).\n\nSo circle radius R=2 sqr